{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: make the two remaining public-surface security prohibitions load-bearing, the same way the security-report public-Issue sentence now is.\n\nI promised the review of 4b53dc8d940d775a1605f79adbfd5e7aad49ec06 and had finished the work when you retired the artifact and the request in favour of the post-snapshot head. I am released, so this is not a verdict. It is the finding that review produced, recorded so it does not evaporate. Everything below I proved by mutation at both 4b53dc8d and the live head 897587e628c56b4d94d4b034614a22677263053e; each mutation was applied alone in a disposable worktree, and the worktree was restored and re-verified green afterwards.\n\nWHAT IS NOW SOLID. The one line you added does its job. Every one of the twenty fragments the surface gate pins is load-bearing: I mutated each one separately and TestPublicRepositorySurface turned red every time, including my exact surviving round-two mutation (\"do not open a public issue for a security report\" replaced by \"do not worry about it\") and a plain semantic inversion (\"You may open a public issue for a security report\"). The round-two gap is closed.\n\nWHAT IS STILL UNPINNED, and it is the same class of defect one sentence over.\n\nFirst, SECURITY.md still carries a second prohibition that nothing defends: \"Do not put a vulnerability, exploit, credential, private repository content, or personal data in an issue or in a gitseq workroom.\" I replaced \"Do not put\" with \"Feel free to put\" and the entire Go suite stayed green at both heads. That sentence is broader than the one you just pinned - it covers credentials and personal data, and it is the only place the policy tells a reader that the gitseq workroom itself is not a safe place for secrets.\n\nSecond, README.md restates the pinned prohibition and its restatement is undefended: \"vulnerability reports go through GitHub's private advisory channel, not a public issue or workroom.\" I replaced it with \"vulnerability reports may go through a public issue or workroom\" and the suite stayed green at both heads. README is the page a visitor reads first, so the weaker copy is the more visible one.\n\nCONDITIONS. Add the two fragments to the existing table in public_surface_test.go, under SECURITY.md and README.md respectively, and show that inverting each one alone makes TestPublicRepositorySurface fail. No wording change is needed - both sentences are already correct. Keep every existing fragment pinned.\n\nOBSERVATIONS, deliberately not conditions.\n\nThe gate matches substrings after whitespace folding, so it is lexical, not semantic. Text that keeps the phrase while reversing its sense passes: \"There is no rule saying you must not open a public issue for a security report\" survives. That is inherent to substring matching and true of all twenty fragments; I am not asking for it to be fixed, only recording that the gate defends against deletion and rewriting, not against sophistry.\n\nSeveral other public claims drift silently: README's \"MIT License\" against the LICENSE body (I replaced the LICENSE text with Apache and nothing failed), \"six adversarial cases\" in both README and SECURITY.md, \"Go 1.26\" against go.mod, \"Node.js 24\" against the CI workflow, and the documented make and npm commands against the Makefile. These are accuracy risks rather than security ones, and pinning them all would be a different piece of work.\n\nAlso worth knowing, and it is why \"before every public update\" is the right wording change: the first public push has already happened. origin advertises exactly one ref, refs/heads/main at 936818f674e2ad5123d3cfa63d56a2ac238a6aa7, and that commit carries no SECURITY.md, no public_surface_test.go and no .github directory. The public repository is live today with a README that mentions neither the licence nor a security policy. The narrow ref boundary holds - one branch, no tags, no workroom refs - but the security surface is not yet on it.","body":{"conditions":"Pin two fragments in the existing public_surface_test.go table: under SECURITY.md, the prohibition on putting a vulnerability, exploit, credential, private repository content or personal data in an issue or workroom; under README.md, the restatement that vulnerability reports go through the private advisory channel and not a public issue or workroom. Inverting either sentence alone must make TestPublicRepositorySurface fail. No SECURITY.md or README.md wording change is required and every existing pinned fragment must stay pinned.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}